Z||000000||GT GT 87||11-10-2001||11:25 PM||||I am installing CW stainless steel headers , w/ studs , and someone had mentioned in the past about useing permatex copper sealant ? I bought this , and it is a silicone sealant, is this rite? It is good to 700* , is this the best stuff to use ?||||reg||1
Z||000001||samoht||11-10-2001||11:42 PM||||unless the head surface is warped, i wouldnt use anything expect the metal gasket..  My opinion||||reg||1
Z||000002||avengador1||11-11-2001||09:47 AM||||You could use it to seal the exhaust gasket, but I wouldn't rely on it as the only seal/gasket. Im not sure but I think the exhaust could get even hotter than 700 degress F especially if you get a vacuum leak.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||GWMORGAN||11-11-2001||10:08 AM||||I installed F.O.C.O.A's headers (jet coated) a little over a year ago and I haven't had any problems. I used the "Ultra copper sealant/gasket maker" made by Permatex, part# 101BR which was supplied by F.O.C.O.A.<P>Remember to use anti-seize compound on the bolts and don't over torque them. Go drive and enjoy. You'll notice the difference!||||reg||14
Z||000004||Nashco||11-11-2001||03:50 PM||||Anytime I do anything removing exhaust parts and reinstalling them, I don't use gaskets.  You'll find that there are *very* few gaskets that will withstand abuse over a long time period, and if so, they are very expensive soft aluminum or copper ones.<P>My dad and I have always used a compound called "Accoustiseal" made by Walker.  It was kind of tough to find, a smaller parts place in town carried it, but I also believe that Napa may carry it.  It comes in a small metal squeeze tube, like the smaller silicone squeeze tubes.  It is black, gooey, tarry like stuff and it works awesome!  We don't use gaskets, and put a slight bead of the accoustiseal on both mating surfaces.  I can honestly say anything we've used this on has never created a leak.  You have to make sure you get both surfaces completely clean (no old gasket stuck to it) and also use quality hardware that won't come loose.<P>I swear by this Accoustiseal stuff...it outperforms any factory gasket, especially if you're dealing with high temps or imperfect parts.  Factory gaskets seem to last less than 5,000 miles with imperfect surfaces...almost seems pointless to put them in there.<P>Good luck....just my experience.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
